groovy has default values, but it doesn't really help, e.g.:

    public MyClass(int number = 1, boolean someFlag = false, boolean anotherFlag = true, ...)
    ...
    // no way to pass only 'anotherFlag'.
    def obj1 = new MyClass(??) 

    // you can pass 'Map', but you will lose compile-time checks
    def obj2 = new MyClass([anotherFlag: true, irrelevantFlag: true])

kotlin is more mature in this aspect, but it has its own problem. scala, lombok, protobuf, etc - there are a lot of different solutions to play with...

however I think the problem lays in concept - **if you need a huge/complex configuration, it should be a separate entity**. this way it will work with any technology. 

while attempts to mix configuration and behaviour within a single entity would lead to the current mess.
